+For changes to a user interface, complete these checks while building:
+
+1. Read the agreed requirements and accepted prototypes. Make a working list of the states and
+   conditions needed to check each affected requirement. Identify what must be visible and usable,
+   and which positions or sizes must stay unchanged. Include controls that reveal additional
+   content or change what is displayed.
+2. In each affected part of the interface, use realistic content that exercises its wrapping,
+   scrolling, and formatting. Vary text length, item count, and content structure where supported.
+   Keep that content in place through interactions that hide or restore it. Combine content and
+   display conditions when they can affect one another.
+3. Follow each affected task through success, failure, and recovery where supported. Exercise
+   recovery by continuing from the failure through correction to completion. Inspect feedback
+   when it appears, changes, and clears, and content when it becomes empty or returns.
+   Observe each change before taking the next action. In automated flows, capture or query the
+   rendered interface after each action that changes content, feedback, or layout. Inspect those
+   observations, including the changed content and surrounding layout.
+4. After repairs, repeat the checks those repairs could affect.
+
